00:12:42,230 --> 00:12:46,310
And then we can also see a little bit more concretely what it means to be an agent.

132
00:12:46,310 --> 00:12:48,550
For example, rolling dice.

133
00:12:48,550 --> 00:12:50,710
That's a huge part of Dn D.

134
00:12:50,710 --> 00:12:55,910
It determines whether or not your attacks hit, whether or not you're able to take certain actions.

135
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:59,990
And it's very easy for a computer to quote unquote roll a dice.

136
00:12:59,990 --> 00:13:05,030
All they need to do is run a little script to generate a random number.

137
00:13:05,030 --> 00:13:07,110
But an LLM is not capable of generating a random number on its own.

138
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:15,110
If you ask it for a random number, the distribution is going to be heavily weighted because it's predicting a likely next token, not a random next token.

139
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,910
Right? So, we can introduce a tool.

140
00:13:17,910 --> 00:13:21,269
Let's say we have a Python function called roll a dice.

141
00:13:21,279 --> 00:13:28,949
And you tell the function how many sides the dice has.

142
00:13:28,949 --> 00:13:43,670
So you would as the game goes on the agent system would very frequently say like oh I as the GM am simulating a monster attacking the player and whenever an attack happens I need to roll a 20sided dice to see whether or not it hits.

143
00:13:43,670 --> 00:13:50,230
So I would call this function roll dice and I need to pass it the argument 20 and that'll be the actual response from the model.

144
00:13:50,230 --> 00:13:54,790
The model won't output the monster hit the player or the monster missed.

145
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:59,189
The model will output, hey, please roll a 20-sided dice.

146
00:13:59,189 --> 00:14:06,310
The agent framework is going to pick that up, do that action, give it back to the model, and then you have a new prompt.

147
00:14:06,310 --> 00:14:15,829
You have a new generation cycle that's going to say, "Okay, it was a 19. The monster hit the player." And then it's going to take that information and speak it out loud.

148
00:14:15,829 --> 00:14:29,430
So you can see how we're very quickly moving to a very complex system where all of these models have to work together in sequence very quickly and they are passing information back

149
00:14:29,430 --> 00:14:36,389
and forth in a sort of contextual memory layer rather than relying on a single prompt to run this entire system end to end.

150
00:14:36,399 --> 00:14:38,870
Yeah.

151
00:14:38,870 --> 00:14:47,990
So composing together different tools and different agents that are specifically capable in particular ways rather than just throwing it at chat GPT and expecting it to,

152
00:14:47,990 --> 00:14:52,471
as you said, generate random numbers, for example, which it's not going to be going to be good at. Right.

153
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:54,230
Exactly.

154
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:59,509
It's always very surprising what capabilities AI models have and don't have.

155
00:14:59,509 --> 00:15:06,550
Sometimes I ask a model to oneshot an entire page of code that I've been thinking about and it just gets it done perfectly on the first try.

156
00:15:06,550 --> 00:15:12,389
But there's other very simple actions that models can't do unless you give it the tools to do so.

157
00:15:12,399 --> 00:15:14,389
Absolutely.

158
00:15:14,389 --> 00:15:16,470
I've attempted generate system just to see what would happen.

159
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:22,389
And you wouldn't be surprised that it's just a big mess by giving it that much all at once. Then of course there's the opposite end of the spectrum.

160
00:15:22,389 --> 00:15:24,230
How many bees are in blueberry?

161
00:15:24,230 --> 00:15:27,750
But you know when you consider token size and things of that nature, people get bent at a shape.

162
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,509
Oh, the AI can't tell you that.

163
00:15:29,509 --> 00:15:31,110
Well, it's a tool.

164
00:15:31,110 --> 00:15:33,990
It may not be able to do that, but it's really good at a bunch of other things.

165
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,150
Exactly.

166
00:15:36,150 --> 00:15:41,750
And you can always use tool calls to address the weakness of a foundation model.

167
00:15:41,750 --> 00:15:54,629
If you give it a Python script that says, "Hey, do this substring matching problem." That's a very simple Python script to implement, it can write that script for you and then run that script for you and then tell you how many Ps are in Blueberry.

168
00:15:54,639 --> 00:16:00,310
You just need to give it the access to the tools in the runtime environment to do that.

169
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:02,231
Yeah. Interesting.

170
00:16:02,231 --> 00:16:11,110
So, there's a few directions we can go, but I want to start with let's say we've gotten this Dungeons and Dragons AI agent to a certain point,

171
00:16:11,110 --> 00:16:15,990
but everything we've done so far, we're using off-the-shelf models.

172
00:16:15,990 --> 00:16:18,550
