173
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:27,829
Yeah, so there's a few places where I see AI teams start to run into some of the limitations of off-the-shelf models.

174
00:16:27,839 --> 00:16:31,829
A big one is domain specific quality.

175
00:16:31,829 --> 00:16:34,629
So when we think about our system,

176
00:16:34,629 --> 00:16:48,069
we are trying to for example have a fun role- playinging voice where it's going to be adventurous and exciting and engaging in the same way that say a book or movie dialogue would be.

177
00:16:48,069 --> 00:16:53,670
Your average off-the-shelf model does not have very much personality like that.

178
00:16:53,670 --> 00:16:59,829
You can get a long way with prompting, but you can always tell when some text is written by a certain model.

179
00:16:59,839 --> 00:17:02,629
Mh.

180
00:17:02,629 --> 00:17:05,029
So, this is even more important, of course, in in real world systems.

181
00:17:05,029 --> 00:17:11,750
I work with a lot of companies in the health care space where they're investing a ton of money in training

182
00:17:11,750 --> 00:17:21,270
and fine-tuning models that do better and do better enough that they can actually be used in the real world for both front and back office healthcare things.

183
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:24,550
It's important in finance. It's important in education.

184
00:17:24,559 --> 00:17:31,990
There are all these places where you want to train or fine-tune a model so that it performs better in your domain.

185
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:38,230
So that's that's the first place is domain specific quality makes people start looking.

186
00:17:38,230 --> 00:17:40,789
The second reason people start looking is latency.

187
00:17:40,789 --> 00:17:53,270
We're now talking about four or five models running and sometimes running multiple calls each in between every single user action.

188
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:55,750
So, I ask the system, you know, hey, my guy swung his sword.

189
00:17:55,750 --> 00:17:58,789
Do you hit the hydra?

190
00:17:58,789 --> 00:18:02,950
And if it's waiting four or 5 seconds to tell me the answer to that, it's not a very good player experience.

191
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:08,950
So, latency is important in literally every single part of AI.

192
00:18:08,950 --> 00:18:13,190
It's especially important in voice AI when people are speaking to a system in real time.

193
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:14,870
And then the third thing is economics.

194
00:18:14,870 --> 00:18:29,270
Like if I'm making this app and it is blowing up and going viral and I am paying linearly more in token costs for every single user who signs up and start using it, it's going to get really expensive really quick.

